# NvChad treats nvim-tree as a window: from the leftmost split, `Ctrl-H`
# (and `Ctrl-W h`, and `Ctrl-W w` when there is only one split) moves
# focus into the file tree, where `j` / `k` / `Enter` browse; `Ctrl-L`
# from the tree comes back to the split. The tree gets a refresh first so
# its rows exist (the files were written after the workspace was
# scanned). `i` → INSERT → Esc proves the editor owns the keys before
# each inbound move: focus is not visible on its own.
write a.txt "alpha\n"
write b.txt "bravo\n"
open a.txt
command editor.use_vim
command view.focus_tree
key r
command view.focus_pane
key i
expect screen contains " INSERT "
key esc
key ctrl+h
key j
key j
key enter
expect pane b.txt
command view.focus_pane
key i
expect screen contains " INSERT "
key esc
key ctrl+w
key h
key k
key enter
expect pane a.txt
command view.focus_pane
key i
expect screen contains " INSERT "
key esc
key ctrl+w
key w
key j
key enter
expect pane b.txt
key ctrl+l
key i
expect screen contains " INSERT "
key esc
